    "id": "notion-biochemistry-0009",
    "subject": "biochemistry",
    "originalNumber": 9,
    "stem": "A 37 year old woman is diagnosed with paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria. Which of the following metabolites would you expect to be increased in her urine?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Urobilinogen"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Conjugated bilirubin"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Bile"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Stercobilin"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria is a hemolytic anemia in which an acquired cell membrane defect increases red blood cell susceptibility to lysis by endogenous complement. Intravascular hemolysis overwhelms hepatic enzyme conjugation capability and leads to an increase in unconjugated bilirubin (water-insoluble) in serum, however, the liver enzyme glucuronyl transferase converts unconjugated bilirubin into conjugated bilirubin (now water-soluble), which is then excreted by hepatocytes into the bile. After secretion of bile into the intestine, ileal and colonic bacteria convert conjugated bilirubin into urobilinogen. A fraction of urobilinogen is oxidized to stercobilin and directly excreted in feces (and hence, would not be a urinary metabolite). The remaining urobilinogen is reabsorbed from the intestine and reenters the portal circulation where a portion is taken up by the liver and re-excreted into bile, while the rest bypasses the liver and is excreted by the kidney. Therefore, in hemolytic anemias, increased liver excretion of conjugated bilirubin will lead to an increase in urinary urobilinogen levels.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria is a hemolytic anemia in which an acquired cell membrane defect increases red blood cell susceptibility to lysis by endogenous complement. Intravascular hemolysis overwhelms hepatic enzyme conjugation capability and leads to an increase in unconjugated bilirubin (water-insoluble) in serum, however, the liver enzyme glucuronyl transferase converts unconjugated bilirubin into conjugated bilirubin (now water-soluble), which is then excreted by hepatocytes into the bile. After secretion of bile into the intestine, ileal and colonic bacteria convert conjugated bilirubin into urobilinogen. A fraction of urobilinogen is oxidized to stercobilin and directly excreted in feces (and hence, would not be a urinary metabolite). The remaining urobilinogen is reabsorbed from the intestine and reenters the portal circulation where a portion is taken up by the liver and re-excreted into bile, while the rest bypasses the liver and is excreted by the kidney. Therefore, in hemolytic anemias, increased liver excretion of conjugated bilirubin will lead to an increase in urinary urobilinogen levels.",
      "B": "Conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble and is the form of bilirubin that is excreted by the liver into bile. It is not typically found in significant amounts in the urine under normal conditions.\nWhile conjugated bilirubin is not a major component of urine, increased intravascular hemolysis in PNH can lead to an increase in unconjugated bilirubin, which is then converted to conjugated bilirubin in the liver.",
      "C": "Bile is produced by the liver and stored in the gallbladder. It contains bile salts, cholesterol, and bilirubin, among other components.\nIncreased intravascular hemolysis in PNH can lead to an increase in bilirubin levels in bile, but bile itself is not typically excreted in significant amounts in the urine.",
      "D": "Stercobilin is a breakdown product of urobilinogen and is responsible for the brown color of feces.\nWhile stercobilin is a product of urobilinogen metabolism in the intestines, it is not typically excreted in urine."
    },
