    "id": "notion-biochemistry-0014",
    "subject": "biochemistry",
    "originalNumber": 14,
    "stem": "Many mutagenic compounds and various forms of radiation lead to mutations through the activity of an error-prone DNA repair mechanism. Which of the following causes mutations by the process of dimerization of adjacent pyrimidines?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Nitrous acid"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Ultraviolet light"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "γ-radiation"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Hydroxylamine"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Pyrimidine dimers are molecular lesions formed from thymine or cytosine bases in DNA via photochemical reactions. Ultraviolet light (UV) induces the formation of covalent linkages between consecutive bases along the nucleotide chain in the vicinity of their carbon–carbon double bonds.\nAdducts between two adjacent pyrimidine bases in a DNA strand comprise more than 95% of the DNA lesions caused by UV light below 340 nm wavelength.\nUltraviolet (UV) light, including that from the sun, produces pyrimidine dimers in DNA that lead to skin cancer.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Nitrous acid can lead to mutations by causing deamination of adenine and cytosine bases. This chemical reaction can change the base-pairing properties of the affected bases.",
      "B": "Pyrimidine dimers are molecular lesions formed from thymine or cytosine bases in DNA via photochemical reactions. Ultraviolet light (UV) induces the formation of covalent linkages between consecutive bases along the nucleotide chain in the vicinity of their carbon–carbon double bonds.\nAdducts between two adjacent pyrimidine bases in a DNA strand comprise more than 95% of the DNA lesions caused by UV light below 340 nm wavelength.\nUltraviolet (UV) light, including that from the sun, produces pyrimidine dimers in DNA that lead to skin cancer.",
      "C": "γ-radiation can cause DNA damage, including single and double-strand breaks, which can lead to mutations. The mechanism involves the ionization of atoms and molecules within the DNA molecule.",
      "D": "Hydroxylamine can induce mutations by causing base substitutions. It modifies DNA bases and may lead to errors during DNA replication."
    },
