    "id": "notion-biochemistry-0033",
    "subject": "biochemistry",
    "originalNumber": 33,
    "stem": "Which of the following ketoacids provides the carbon skeleton for an amino acid capable of carrying 2 atoms of nitrogen per molecule from other tissues to the liver and kidney via the blood?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Oxaloacetate"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Alpha Ketoglutarate"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Alpha Ketoisocaproic Acid"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Acetoacetate"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "α-Ketoglutarate is capable of accepting 2 moles of ammonia, first via the action of glutamate dehydrogenase yielding glutamate and then via glutamine synthetase yielding glutamine. Glutamate plays the central role in mammalian nitrogen flow, serving as both a nitrogen donor and nitrogen acceptor. Glutamine is the nitrogen repository found in the circulatory system. Its role there is to carry ammonia to and from various tissues but principally from peripheral tissues to the kidney.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Oxaloacetate is a four-carbon dicarboxylic acid.\nIt is a key intermediate in the citric acid cycle (Krebs cycle) and is involved in energy production.\nHowever, it is not directly involved in carrying nitrogen atoms.",
      "B": "α-Ketoglutarate is capable of accepting 2 moles of ammonia, first via the action of glutamate dehydrogenase yielding glutamate and then via glutamine synthetase yielding glutamine. Glutamate plays the central role in mammalian nitrogen flow, serving as both a nitrogen donor and nitrogen acceptor. Glutamine is the nitrogen repository found in the circulatory system. Its role there is to carry ammonia to and from various tissues but principally from peripheral tissues to the kidney.",
      "C": "Alpha Ketoisocaproic Acid is a ketoacid derived from leucine.\nWhile leucine is an essential amino acid, its ketoacid does not serve as a primary carrier of nitrogen atoms in the bloodstream.",
      "D": "Acetoacetate is a ketone body produced during the breakdown of fatty acids.\nIt is not directly involved in carrying nitrogen atoms."
    },
