    "id": "notion-biochemistry-0034",
    "subject": "biochemistry",
    "originalNumber": 34,
    "stem": "Which of the forces or interactions play the predominant role in driving RNA secondary and tertiary structure formation?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Hydrophilic repulsion"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Hydrophobic interaction"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Salt bridge formation"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Formation of complementary base pair regions"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "The secondary structure involves alpha-helices, beta sheets, and other types of folding patterns that occur due to a regular repeating pattern of hydrogen bond formation. The tertiary structure involves electrostatic and hydrophobic interactions, van der Waals interactions, and hydrogen and disulfide bonds. Hydrophobic amino acid residues tend to collect in the interior of globular proteins, where they exclude water.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Hydrophilic repulsion is not a significant factor in driving RNA secondary or tertiary structure.\nRNA structures are primarily stabilized by specific interactions like hydrogen bonding and base stacking.",
      "B": "The secondary structure involves alpha-helices, beta sheets, and other types of folding patterns that occur due to a regular repeating pattern of hydrogen bond formation. The tertiary structure involves electrostatic and hydrophobic interactions, van der Waals interactions, and hydrogen and disulfide bonds. Hydrophobic amino acid residues tend to collect in the interior of globular proteins, where they exclude water.",
      "C": "Salt bridge formation involves the attraction between positively and negatively charged amino acid residues.\nWhile salt bridges are important in protein folding, they are less prevalent in RNA structures.",
      "D": "The formation of complementary base pair regions is a key driver of RNA secondary structure.\nIn RNA, base pairing (A-U, G-C) leads to the formation of secondary structures such as stem-loops, hairpins, and bulges.\nThe tertiary structure further arises from interactions like stacking of adjacent base pairs, coaxial stacking, and long-range interactions between distant regions."
    },
