    "id": "notion-biochemistry-0055",
    "subject": "biochemistry",
    "originalNumber": 55,
    "stem": "Which statement about metabolism of sugar is CORRECT?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Galactose is phosphorylated to galactose-1-phosphate by galactokinase"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Sucrose can be biosynthesized from galactose and fructose by the liver"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Fructose is an aldose sugar-like glucose"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Fructose transport into cells is insulin independent"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "This is correct.\nFructose can enter cells via the insulin-independent GLUT5 fructose transporter. In hepatocytes, fructose is phosphorylated to fructose-1-phosphate (F1P) by the enzyme fructokinase.\nNearly 90% of the dietary fructose is metabolized by the liver. Fructose undergoes more rapid glycolysis in the liver than does glucose because it bypasses the regulatory step catalyzed by phosphofructokinase (Figure 20–5). This allows fructose to flood the pathways in the liver, leading to increased fatty acid synthesis, esterification of fatty acids, and secretion of very-low-density lipoprotein (VLDL), which may raise serum triacylglycerols and ultimately raise LDL cholesterol concentrations.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "This statement is incorrect.\nGalactose is phosphorylated to galactose-1-phosphate by galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase, not galactokinase.",
      "B": "This statement is incorrect.\nSucrose is a disaccharide composed of glucose and fructose, not galactose and fructose. It is not synthesized in the liver but rather in plants and transported in the diet.",
      "C": "This statement is incorrect.\nFructose is a ketose sugar, while glucose is an aldose sugar. They differ in the location of their carbonyl group (C=O) in the linear form.",
      "D": "This is correct.\nFructose can enter cells via the insulin-independent GLUT5 fructose transporter. In hepatocytes, fructose is phosphorylated to fructose-1-phosphate (F1P) by the enzyme fructokinase.\nNearly 90% of the dietary fructose is metabolized by the liver. Fructose undergoes more rapid glycolysis in the liver than does glucose because it bypasses the regulatory step catalyzed by phosphofructokinase (Figure 20–5). This allows fructose to flood the pathways in the liver, leading to increased fatty acid synthesis, esterification of fatty acids, and secretion of very-low-density lipoprotein (VLDL), which may raise serum triacylglycerols and ultimately raise LDL cholesterol concentrations."
    },
