    "id": "notion-biochemistry-0080",
    "subject": "biochemistry",
    "originalNumber": 80,
    "stem": "Which statement about carbohydrates metabolism is CORRECT?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Glycogen is stored mainly in the liver and brain"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Insulin inhibits the biosynthesis of glycogen"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Glycogen can be broken down to glucose-6-phosphate in muscle which then release free glucose by the action of the enzyme glucose-6-phosphatase"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "A key step in the biosynthesis of glycogen is the formation of UDP-glucose"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "This statement is correct. The formation of UDP-glucose is a crucial step in glycogen biosynthesis. UDP-glucose serves as the activated donor of glucose residues during the elongation of the glycogen chain. The enzyme UDP-glucose pyrophosphorylase catalyzes the synthesis of UDP-glucose from glucose 1-phosphate and UTP.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "This statement is incorrect. The main stores of glycogen are found in skeletal muscle and the liver, with the liver being a significant storage site. While the brain does contain glycogen, the amount is relatively low compared to skeletal muscle and the liver.",
      "B": "This statement is incorrect. Insulin promotes the biosynthesis of glycogen. When insulin levels are elevated, it signals the body to increase glycogenesis (the synthesis of glycogen) and decrease glycogenolysis (the breakdown of glycogen).",
      "C": "This statement is partly correct. In muscle cells, glycogen can be broken down to glucose-6-phosphate through glycogenolysis. However, muscle cells lack glucose-6-phosphatase, so they cannot release free glucose into the bloodstream. The released glucose-6-phosphate is used for energy within the muscle cells.",
      "D": "This statement is correct. The formation of UDP-glucose is a crucial step in glycogen biosynthesis. UDP-glucose serves as the activated donor of glucose residues during the elongation of the glycogen chain. The enzyme UDP-glucose pyrophosphorylase catalyzes the synthesis of UDP-glucose from glucose 1-phosphate and UTP."
    },
