    "id": "notion-biochemistry-0087",
    "subject": "biochemistry",
    "originalNumber": 87,
    "stem": "A patient presented with dysmorphia and cerebellar degeneration. Analysis of his blood indicated elevated levels of phytanic acid and very long-chain fatty acids but no elevation of palmitate. His symptoms are consistent with a defect in an enzyme involved in which of the following?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Degradation of mucopolysaccharides"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Transport of enzymes into lysosomes"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Elongation of fatty acids"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Oxidation"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "The elevated levels of phytanic acid and very long-chain fatty acids suggest a defect in the elongation of fatty acids. Phytanic acid is a branched-chain fatty acid, and the accumulation of such fatty acids is indicative of a peroxisomal disorder, such as Refsum disease.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The symptoms described (dysmorphia and cerebellar degeneration) and the elevated levels of phytanic acid and very long-chain fatty acids are not indicative of a defect in the degradation of mucopolysaccharides. Mucopolysaccharidoses are a group of disorders related to the deficiency of lysosomal enzymes involved in the degradation of mucopolysaccharides.",
      "B": "The symptoms and biochemical findings do not align with a defect in the transport of enzymes into lysosomes. Lysosomal storage disorders could be related to impaired enzyme transport, but the specific markers mentioned (phytanic acid and very long-chain fatty acids) are not associated with lysosomal enzyme transport.",
      "C": "The elevated levels of phytanic acid and very long-chain fatty acids suggest a defect in the elongation of fatty acids. Phytanic acid is a branched-chain fatty acid, and the accumulation of such fatty acids is indicative of a peroxisomal disorder, such as Refsum disease.",
      "D": "The symptoms and biochemical findings are consistent with a defect in fatty acid oxidation. Phytanic acid is metabolized in peroxisomes through alpha-oxidation, and the accumulation of very long-chain fatty acids suggests impaired peroxisomal function related to fatty acid oxidation.\nHowever, the absence of elevated palmitate levels indicates that the defect is specifically related to the elongation of fatty acids, rather than their initial synthesis or degradation."
    },
