      "A": "Superoxide dismutase catalyzes the dismutation of the superoxide ion (O2-) into molecular oxygen (O2) and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2). The reaction it catalyzes is:\n⁍\nIt doesn't directly catalyze the reaction mentioned in the question.",
      "B": "Peroxidase is an enzyme that catalyzes the reaction ⁍.\nThis reaction involves the conversion of Fe2+ (ferrous ion) to Fe3+ (ferric ion) and the reduction of molecular oxygen (O) to water (HO). The peroxidase enzyme facilitates this reaction by providing a suitable environment for the transfer of electrons and the formation of the products. It acts as a catalyst, speeding up the reaction without being consumed in the process. Therefore, peroxidase is the enzyme that catalyzes this specific reaction.",
      "C": "Cytochrome c oxidase is involved in the electron transport chain in the inner mitochondrial membrane. It catalyzes the reduction of molecular oxygen (O2) to water (H2O), but the specific reaction it catalyzes is not the same as the one in the question.",
      "D": "Cytochrome P450 enzymes are involved in the metabolism of various compounds, including drugs. They are not directly involved in the reaction mentioned."
