    "id": "notion-biochemistry-0091",
    "subject": "biochemistry",
    "originalNumber": 91,
    "stem": "Individuals who develop scurvy suffer from sore and bleeding gums and loss of teeth. This is a result of synthesis of a defective collagen molecule. The step that is affected in collagen biosynthesis attributable to scurvy is which of the following?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "The formation of lysyl cross links between collagen molecules"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "The formation of disulfide bonds, which initiates tropocollagen formation"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "The hydroxylation of proline residues, which stabilizes the collagen structure"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "The formation of collagen fibrils"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "This is the correct step affected in scurvy. Vitamin C is essential for the activity of prolyl hydroxylase, an enzyme that hydroxylates proline residues in collagen. Hydroxylation of proline is critical for stabilizing the collagen triple helix structure.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The formation of lysyl cross-links is crucial for the stability and strength of collagen fibrils. Lysyl oxidase catalyzes the cross-linking of lysine residues in collagen molecules, contributing to the integrity of the collagen structure. However, this step is not directly affected in scurvy.",
      "B": "Disulfide bonds are not the primary bonds involved in collagen formation. Collagen is initially synthesized as procollagen, where three polypeptide chains wind around each other to form a triple helix (tropocollagen). The disulfide bonds do play a role in stabilizing the structure but are not the primary initiators.",
      "C": "This is the correct step affected in scurvy. Vitamin C is essential for the activity of prolyl hydroxylase, an enzyme that hydroxylates proline residues in collagen. Hydroxylation of proline is critical for stabilizing the collagen triple helix structure.",
      "D": "Collagen fibrils are formed by the aggregation of collagen molecules with proper cross-linking. While lysyl cross-links contribute to fibril formation, the primary issue in scurvy is the defective collagen molecules due to the lack of hydroxylation of proline residues."
    },
