    "id": "superexam-biochemistry-0621",
    "subject": "biochemistry",
    "originalNumber": 621,
    "stem": "A 49 year old man with a 10 year history of poorly\ncontrolled diabetes mellitus presents to his physician\ncomplaining of changes in his vision. Physical\nexamination reveals opacities on the lens of the eye.\nWhich enzyme most likely contributed to this\ncomplication?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Adenosine deaminase"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Aldose reductase"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Hexokinase"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Glucokinase"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Aldose reductase catalyzes the breakdown of glucose\ninto sorbitol. Sorbitol is then metabolized to fructose.\nIn patients with hyperglycemia as in a case of DM,\nsorbitol accumulation with the cells leads in the rise in\nthe intracellular osmolality, causing water movement\nin the cell resulting to osmolar swelling and osmotic\ndamage. swelling of the lens fiber can lead into\nrupture and cataract formation.",
